(*!
<summary>Creates independent enumerators for traversing a collection.</summary>
<description>
The collection is borrowed by its enumerators and must remain alive throughout their use.
Enumerator ownership is separate from ownership of the collection and its elements.
</description>
*)

INTERFACE IEnumerable EXTENDS IObject]]></Declaration>
    <Method Name="CreateEnumerator" Id="{0977fb9b-9046-4f07-b4fd-b067d11a88d4}">
      <Declaration><![CDATA[(*!
<summary>Creates a new dynamically allocated enumerator with its own traversal state.</summary>
<description>
On successful creation, the caller owns the enumerator and must explicitly release it after use.
The returned interface refers to that instance; copying the interface does not copy the enumerator.
Each call creates an independent cursor positioned before the first element.
The enumerator is not registered for automatic cycle cleanup and may be used across PLC cycles
while its source collection remains alive and its traversal contract is respected.
Releasing the enumerator does not release its source collection or transfer ownership of its elements.
Allocation failure invokes the shared terminal allocation-failure mechanism without creating a heap exception.
A null enumerator is not a recoverable failure result. The application boundary must enforce the fatal latch.
</description>
*)
METHOD CreateEnumerator : IEnumerator]]></Declaration>
    </Method>
  </Itf>
</TcPlcObject>